From 05883c2e3c347df21ef8202d92812674abb416c6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 09:05:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] v3.1.7: firewall table walker, R15 restore race, WG/PBR, 3
 field fixes

Firewall table walker (reaper_fwsim)
  Each feature owns witness packets; the walker pushes them through the LIVE
  tables in kernel order after every firewall apply and names the rule that
  decided each one. Report-only - nothing here changes a rule.
  - reaper_fwsim/{reaper_fwsim.c,Makefile,witness.static}, wired like rchqd
  - reaper_fwsim_kick() at the tail of start_firewall (delay 3), in
    reaper_fw apply/confirm/rollback, and after the PBR apply (delay 1)
  - rwatch 3g: FAIL witness-red, one log line per CHANGE of the red set
  - reaper_diag 14g; httpd reaper_fw.cgi?action=witness[&run=1]
  - www Rule Status tab + the arm card's "N red" preview
  - rmcpd get_firewall_status
  Metal: 80 witnesses, 72 green / 0 red / 8 n/a on the RT-BE96U, matching the
  private fixture; sub-second over 594 v4 + 350 v6 rules.

R15 - filter restore refused on an RT-BE88U
  Root cause is a RACE, not a bad ruleset: this iptables 1.4.x has no xtables
  lock, so xt_replace_table() returns EAGAIN when another process changes the
  table between the *filter snapshot and COMMIT - and nat-start is FORKED by
  start_nat_rules() inside nat_setting(), which runs BEFORE filter_setting().
  reaper_restore_run() re-runs the identical file up to 5x (100/200/400/800 ms)
  on a COMMIT-line failure before hoisting, probing or dropping; the same loop
  guards the nat restore.

Walker discoverability (BACKLOG P2)
  A clean walk is silent, so a red witness could stand unseen. The dashboard
  Security card and the Firewall Status tab now both report it, reading the
  CACHED report (action=witness, no run=1) - rwatch already re-walks every
  5 min, so neither surface ever triggers a walk. No new dict tokens.

Field fixes
  1. Gatekeeper: a removed device re-appeared under Pending Approvals.
     "pending = seen but not in gk_rl" had no presence gate, and gkd RETAINS a
     device in seen.tsv for 24 h after its last sighting - so removing anything
     seen in the last day was undone by the next 5 s poll. A reboot appeared to
     fix it only because seen.tsv is on tmpfs. Pending now requires the device
     to be online in the current sweep or seen within GK_PEND_TTL (15 min);
     retention for the approved-device list is untouched. The online flag is
     tested separately because gkd leaves last=0 before the first NTP sync.
  2/3. "Applying Settings" hung forever on a VLAN/SDN change.
     Every SDN apply runs showLoading() with no countdown and only starts the
     10..100% bar from httpApi.nvramSet's SUCCESS callback - and that ajax
     carried `error: function(){}`. The apply breaks its own reply (restart_sdn
     /restart_net bounce the network under the in-flight request), so whether
     the response survives is a race: hence "random", and hence the countup
     sometimes starting minutes later. dataType:'json' widens it - a truncated
     body is an error, not a success. The caller contract is deliberately
     unchanged (75 call sites are written against success-only semantics);
     instead the error path now recovers the UI, polling httpd_check.xml until
     httpd answers and then reloading, and the async path gets a timeout.

Gates: 10/10 host suites (incl. test_fwsim tier 1 + the private tier 2),
static checks 5/5, csrfcheck 0 breakers over 64 sites / 16 gated endpoints.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
